  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 244 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nOn Saturday, the upper-level ridge and surface high pressure center \nwill finally shift more quickly eastward as an upper-level trough \ncrosses the Rocky Mountains into the Great Plains. This evolution \nwill provide a more appreciable increase in moisture across the Mid-\nMississippi River Valley including low-level moisture from the \nnortheastern Gulf of Mexico. There is expected to be a corresponding \nincrease in cloud cover on Saturday, which should lead to high \ntemperatures similar to Friday (around 70 to mid-70s F) with \npersisting low-level WAA or perhaps slightly cooler if clouds \nincrease more quickly.\n\nThe aforementioned trough will continue eastward Saturday night into \nSunday, forcing a cold front through the CWA. Ensemble model \nguidance probabilities of measurable rainfall continue to increase \nto near 90+ percent with this front as well as now peak Sunday \nmorning with a trend toward the trough passing directly overhead in \naddition to a stronger and later-arriving front. It is worth noting \nthat these 24-hourly probabilities are much higher than the 50 to 80 \npercent 6-hourly PoPs in the official forecast due to some timing \nuncertainties and the NBM still catching the increasing trend. The \nhighest probabilities of 0.25\"+ rainfall are 40 to 60 percent across \ncentral/northeastern MO and west-central IL, with probabilities \nlowering toward the east since large-scale ascent may begin \nweakening as the trough encounters ridging sandwiched between it and \na potential developing subtropical or tropical system near the \nCarolinas. Due to the uncertainties in frontal timing, the NBM \ninterquartile range is around 10 F on Sunday, but confidence is \nincreasing that temperatures will cool to near or below average next \nweek as a strong surface high pressure center enters the north-\ncentral CONUS.",
